               <head>Legal and documentary formularies; England, mid-15th century</head>
               <msContents>
                  <msItem xml:id="MS_Lat_misc_e_103-item1">
                     <locus from="1" to="17" type="inferredEnd">(fols. 1r-17v)</locus>
                     <title key="work_14465">Curia baronis: rules for holding courts with specimen pleas</title>
                     <rubric xml:lang="la">Curia baronis et eciam regalis</rubric>
                     <incipit>En primez coment quen lune curte</incipit>
                     <explicit>vel non debet detinet vel non detinet</explicit>
                     <note>The initials W.K. [William Kingsmill ?] occur frequently in the pleas. </note>
                     <bibl>Baker &amp; Ringrose, pp. 126-7, and cf. pp. 619-20</bibl>
                     <textLang mainLang="la" otherLangs="xno">Latin and Anglo-Norman</textLang>
                     <!-- not in Dean & Boulton -->
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem xml:id="MS_Lat_misc_e_103-item2">
                     <locus from="18" to="78r" type="inferredEnd">(fols. 18r-69r)</locus>
                     <author key="person_1275">William Kingsmill</author>
                     <title key="work_15008">De forma et compositione cartarum</title>
                     <incipit>Tria sunt que pertinent ad cartam</incipit>
                     <finalRubric><locus from="69r" to="69r">(fol. 69r)</locus> Explicit cartuaria de munimentis sufficienter secundum legem temporalem regni Anglie etc.</finalRubric>
                     <note>Ends in the section on wills; the final document is an acquitance of John Kirkeby dated at Lincoln 1 September 1443. The text and examples are identical with those of MS. Lat. misc. e. 93 as far as fol. 51v (= Lat. misc. e. 93, fol. 38 Indentura apprentecietatis). The latest date is 1446 (fol.69). Most of the wills given are dated 1443. A second hand has added in the text (fol.45v–52) dates between 1443 and 1458.</note>
                     <bibl>Baker &amp; Ringrose, pp. 33-34</bibl>
                     <textLang mainLang="la" otherLangs="enm xno">Latin with a little Middle English (fols. 54v, 59r-v) and Anglo-Norman (fol. 54v)</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem xml:id="MS_Lat_misc_e_103-item3">
                     <locus>(fols. 69r–78r, 85v–97r)</locus>
                     <note>Additional examples, dated 1446–62 (two in English fol.90v, 91), mostly relating to <placeName key="place_7008171">Yorkshire</placeName>
                     </note>
                     <textLang mainLang="la" otherLangs="enm">Latin and Middle English</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem xml:id="MS_Lat_misc_e_103-item3b">
                     <locus from="78v" to="79r">(fols. 78v–79r)</locus>
                     <author key="person_105557713">Thomas Sampson</author>
                     <title key="work_7565">Modus faciendi testamentum</title>
                     <rubric>Regule que requiruntur antequam fiat testamentum</rubric>
                     <incipit>In cuiuslibet testamenti exordio duo sunt principalia consederanda</incipit>
                     <explicit>ubicumque deus disposuerit etc.</explicit>
                     <bibl>Baker &amp; Ringrose, p. 202; H. G. Richardson, 'Business Training in Medieval Oxford', <title>The American Historical Review</title>, vol. 46, no. 2, 1941, pp. 259–80 at 279</bibl>
                     <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem xml:id="MS_Lat_misc_e_103-item4">
                     <locus from="79v" to="85">(fol.79v–85r)</locus>
                     <author key="person_105557713">Thomas Sampson</author>
                     <title key="work_7566">Regulae de cartis componendis </title>
                     <rubric>Regule de cartis componendis</rubric>
                     <incipit>In primis sciendum est quid sit carta. Carta est quoddam feoffamentum sive scriptum</incipit>
                     <explicit>satis apparet in forma in composicionibus</explicit>
                     <note>De cartis, de scriptis, de obligationibus. No specimens.</note>
                     <bibl>Baker &amp; Ringrose, p. 201; Richardson, 'Business training', 278</bibl>
                     <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem xml:id="MS_Lat_misc_e_103-item5">
                     <locus from="97v">(fol.97v)</locus>
                     <title key="work_15437">Verses</title>
                     <incipit>Femina pulcra foris multis est cause doloris</incipit>
                     <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem>
                     <locus>(fol. 37r, margin)</locus>
                     <incipit>Jhesus be my spede in grace and vertu to to procede</incipit>
                     <note>Two couplets (?) of English verse added, 16th century (not noted in the Folger First Line Index).</note>
                     <textLang mainLang="en">English</textLang>
                  </msItem>
